sis  t is a phonetic complement meaning that  it repeats the second  of the biliteral sounds and then an egg  which is a determinative that can be  used in the new kingdom  for the name of a goddess so that is  isis  another biliteral where which is the  bird  w-r followed by its phonetic complement  and then if we keep going again we see  the stacking  of signs for the word pet so the p  and the t reading across and then  the pet sign itself which is used both  as an ideogram  and a determinative  if we move to the next figure our green  faced osiris here we again see that the  hieroglyphs are facing the same way  as osiris we have the i and the set sign  that writes wessier the ancient name for  osiris we  typically use the hellenized the greek  version  and the seated god is the determinative  so osiris then a tri-literal  genti which means foremost and he is  foremost of the yementiu  the westerners now you meant you  is plural which is why we have three  strokes and when we get into the grammar  of nouns  we will talk about singular dual  and plural osiris has two additional  epithets  when nefer spelled with the desert hair  and the nefer sign and nebta  jesser lord of the sacred land with then  the three-hilled khasat sign the desert  sign  as the determinative so the two gods  facing to the right have their  hieroglyphs facing  the same direction as they are  when we turn next to king seti the first  we see the hieroglyphs change  orientation so that  now the label to seti is facing the same  way  as the king we see seti in this camp  crown  with urayus and the hieroglyphs that  label  him face left to right  just as he does and this says a  spell foreseen the god  this is part of the daily ritual  and then jed medu in  words spoken by and this is another  great example of you read the  je serpent first then the medustic  then the read leaf so every time you  come to  stacked signs you keep reading in the  overall direction  and then left to right across  or topped down depending on how they're  stacked  these are words spoken by the king and  the king is  neb tawi lord of the two lands  men ma'at ra that is the name of seti  the first  and you will notice that this cartouche  i read  bottom to top this is another principle  of hieroglyphic writing  called honorific transposition  if you have a word or a name that  includes the name of the god  or sometimes also in reference to the  king you  write the god's name first even though  you read it  last so you read min first  enduring ma'at  cosmic order of rey  so that is an example where the  orientation the order of the signs  is different than what you would expect  because of honorific transposition  so let's look at this introductory  section  on one of the sides of the second shrine  of tutankhamun  from the tomb of tutankhamun this is  based on  a segment of the otherwise better known  book of gates  so here we see two goddesses to either  side of a ram-headed pole  the ram head indicates youth  or flesh the night form of the sun god  and this goddess is adoring  as is this goddess this head of rey and  in fact it's labeled here  in more or less normal orthography  head of ra now these are the names  of the two goddesses now notice this  this isn't a common sign  it's a very uncommon sign it's a person  with their arms out  surrounded by a series of dots they're  swimming  this is the elaborate determinative  uh we might even say idiogram of to swim  which is nebi in the egyptian the verb  nevi  so it is followed by a cha  hobble so i've got nebich  then i've got a second age so aha  plosive h normal hieroglyph followed by  these three pronged  plant signs that can appear as  determinatives they can have the  phonetic value  hen but remember we mentioned  substitution  i can say well you know what i have a  plant sign here so i'm going to  substitute a plant sign  each of these substitutes for the  standard  read leaf the value yod or  can transcribe it almost as an i even so  this is an attested orthography of the  name  nebuchut that we know from the  hellenized form  much better as neftes so  this is a cryptographic orthography of  the name of the goddess nephews  and if we see nephews and we see another  goddess we can be fairly certain that  this  is in fact the goddess isis and it is  two shah signs shesh but  the first rectangular sign  which is a land or water sign can  substitute for another this can  substitute for the  island sign that has rounded edges  that has the phonetic value u and of  course the  wow being a weak consonant we can  disregard if we want to in cryptography  and sha remember our substitution i can  substitute one bird sign for another if  i want to i can play around with signs  based on their phonetic classification  sha  is a sibilant so it can substitute for  another  s so here we've got rather than shash  we've got you ish for is for us  isa for isis so we have isis and nephews  the head of rey  and cryptographically here men  the red crown net for n actually appears  in normal hieroglyphic text  a lot in ramisit period  nan m that's a sober substituting for  the  m owl m sa here  the elaborate we might say piece of  cloth with a belt  substituting just for that bolt of cloth  that's the normal s  so another substitution of kind nan m  secchiere pan the spewing mouth  for poo for poi pee  um it's like bilabial it literally is  demonstrating the plosive action of the  mouth and then  m sec your pen these are like this  menom secure pen  these are like this these exist in this  manner these gods are like this  this is a common introductory annotation  in the netherlands books  men and secure pen probably sheptep  receiving the head or those who receive  the head and then it says  if we look at these signs they're single  hieroglyphs so they're probably  substituting for single phonetic values  we've got um an n new for n  we have our saw bird that could be  standing for any  single uniliteral bird sign here's  probably an  m here we've got our three-pond  plant substituting for some plant sign  ah there's our elaborate say cloth with  the belt  so let's assume that might be s again  um and then there's a snake  now a snake can substitute for another  snake so here's an elaborate serpent  that usually  appears as a determinative what if it's  substituting for the horn viper  don't mean an f that would be an f now  if i read this  ness f something right well already  that's not not working doesn't what does  that mean well  what if it's retrograde then so um  that's  so i worked with this for a while then i  realized well  um an annotation that often appears with  deities that will appear with the king  etc is a protective  formula that describes life protection  etc being  all around someone and egyptian has this  interesting preposition  ha second h olive that literally means  around behind so it gets across this  notion of being around  not just behind as we often translate  it's like the wings of the horus falcon  in that great statue of khafra in the  cairo museum  ha yeah you're around behind so so what  i realized is  this is a really interesting use of  perturbation  so that when i read this text i am doing  what the goddesses are doing  it is sen em  ha this three pawn plant substituting  for the  ha sign three prime plant which is  something that we find  actually happening sometimes in normal  hieroglyphic texts based on  mistranscriptions out of the hierarchic  remember some of these signs look a lot  more similar in the cursive  than they look in the hieroglyphs so  some of the substitutions  in the cryptography are playing with the  possibilities out of hierarchy and then  transposing those into hieroglyphs  sen am ha f i read this circularly  they are all around him meaning in this  protective  gesture so the direction of reading of  hieroglyphs also has a magical aspect  to it reading in retrograde  reading in a circular fashion etc this  is  more for an ancient egyptian than just a  you know it's not a tongue twister this  is more than an eye  twister this is more than just messing  with something this can  literally cause you to make the  motion of backing up of going  back around something and the retrograde  motion  can relate to the concept of enchantment  for the egyptians  which is shiny pressure etc to go around  to encircle so retrograde writing  and perturbations mixing up of signs for  the ancient egyptians  can impart an even deeper or additional  meaning to a text  so we hope you enjoyed the episode today  from  simple orientation to some of the most  complex perturbation and cryptography  that there really is  in any ancient egyptian text  if you want to follow our vintage and  egyptological adventures  don't forget to give us a follow on  vintage egyptologists  on instagram if you haven't subscribed  yet here on youtube  please do so uh give us a few comments  suggestions for future episodes and  we'll make sure to include in the notes  references to all the books we're using  as well as where you can find more  information about how to read  hieroglyphs  [Music]  i

